Context window limits??

Definition and Core Understanding
Context window limit refers to the maximum amount of text (tokens) that an AI language model can process or "remember" at once during a single interaction or inference. It defines the boundary of how much input the model can consider when generating a response.

Token: A token can be a word, part of a word, or even punctuation depending on the tokenizer used.
Context Window: The sequence of tokens the model attends to simultaneously.
For example, if a model has a context window limit of 4,096 tokens, it can only consider the last 4,096 tokens of the conversation or input text when generating the next output.

Step-by-Step Explanation
Tokenization:
The input text is split into tokens using a tokenizer. For example, the sentence "Hello world!" might become ["Hello", "world", "!"].

Context Window Size:
The model has a fixed maximum number of tokens it can process at once. This is a hard architectural limit determined by the model design (e.g., GPT-3's base models have 2,048 tokens, GPT-4 can have 8,192 or more).

Attention Mechanism:
Transformers use self-attention to attend to all tokens within the context window simultaneously. This means the model "remembers" and uses all tokens within this window to generate coherent and contextually relevant output.

Truncation or Sliding Window:
If the input exceeds the context window size, the oldest tokens are truncated or discarded, or a sliding window approach is used to keep the most recent tokens.

Impact on Model Output:

If important context is outside the window, the model cannot use it, potentially causing loss of coherence or forgetting earlier conversation details.
Longer context windows enable better understanding of long documents or conversations.
